{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["The sense of <b>לְכָל הָאֹתֹת</b> is joined with 'and there did not arise a prophet again in Israel like Moses for all the signs,' or one may say that HaShem knew him face to face through signs and wonders, all of which were performed by His great Name, as it says (Deuteronomy 4:37), 'And He brought you out before Him with His great power.' I have already explained (Ramban on Deuteronomy 13:2) that the אֹתוֹת are those things regarding which He gave warning in advance, as in 'tomorrow this sign shall be' (Exodus 8:19), and it says concerning any matter that the prophet declares in advance, saying 'thus it will come and come to pass in the future,' and the מוֹפְתִים are things done through a change in the natural order, such as the staff that turned into a serpent (Exodus 7:9). Now signs and wonders were performed for other prophets as well, such as Elijah and Elisha, who performed many wonders, but the distinction of Moses our teacher regarding them — the Sages said in the midrash: all the prophets would perform miracles by means of prayer, like the sun for Joshua, and the son of the Zarephath woman, and the withholding of rain for Elijah, and the son of the Shunammite woman for Elisha, but Moses would perform them immediately. This midrash is not clear. The Rav said in the Guide for the Perplexed (II:35) that the distinction of Moses our teacher is what Scripture says, <b>to Pharaoh, to all his servants, and to all his land</b>, and <b>before the eyes of all Israel</b>, for they were before the eyes of those who opposed him and those who agreed with him, whereas the other prophets would perform them for individuals among men, as in the matter stated (II Kings 8:4), 'Tell me, please, all the great things that Elisha has done.' This is not correct in my eyes, for the withholding of rain by Elijah — both the withholding and the bringing — became very widely known, and it was also by his own decree, 'As YHWH lives, there shall not be dew nor rain these years except by my word' (I Kings 17:1), and as the matter continued for three years it became greatly revealed and heard among the nations, and it is written, 'Is there a nation or kingdom where my master has not sent...' (I Kings 18:10), and the conclusion of that matter is revealed and publicized to those who opposed him and to those who agreed with him in a single assembly, as it is written (I Kings 18:19), 'Gather to me all Israel to Mount Carmel and the prophets of Baal, four hundred...' And likewise the standing of the sun for Joshua was truly before the eyes of all Israel, and all the kings of Canaan who fought against him and who remained saw it, and our Sages (Genesis Rabbah 6:14) recall likewise that they saw it from one end of the world to the other, and Scripture distinguishes it greatly (Joshua 10:14), 'And there was no day like that before it or after it, that YHWH listened to the voice of a man.' And in the splitting of the Jordan and in the splitting of the Sea of Reeds, it was not done for individuals but for all Israel, and it is written further concerning it (Joshua 5:1), 'And it came to pass when all the kings of the Amorites who were across the Jordan heard, and all the kings of the Canaanites, that YHWH dried up the waters of the Jordan.' But according to the plain sense of Scripture, everything is tied and joined together: it means that there did not arise a prophet again in Israel like Moses, whom HaShem knew face to face, and to perform signs and wonders with a strong hand and great awe like Moses, and if some of them performed some of these, they did not reach the multitude of them nor the great degree that was in them, for there was nothing like the day of Sinai before it or after it, and likewise the signs of the prophets did not reach the great duration that his reached, peace be upon him, for the sign of the manna was continuous for forty years, and the pillar of fire and cloud, also the well and the quail according to the opinion of our Sages (Ta'anit 9a), and their journeying through the great and awesome wilderness — serpent, scorpion, and thirst where there is no water — yet they lacked nothing. And by way of truth, one may say that his signs and wonders of Moses were face to face, as I have explained (Ramban on Deuteronomy 34:10)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {0 "The closing words 'his signs and wonders of Moses' read awkwardly in the Hebrew; translated hyper-literally as printed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:00:34.094167725Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}